keywords:
  - "Riemann Xi-function"
  - "harmonic nodal curves"
  - "Arias de Reyna X-ray"
  - "Csordas-Smith-Varga velocity"
  - "Laguerre expression"
  - "transverse Wronskian"
  - "hyperbolic sinc kernel"
abstract: >-
  For any entire function f(t - i*d) = R(d,t) + i*I(d,t) real on R, Cauchy-Riemann orthogonality
  gives d|R|/ds = +/-|grad I| = +/-|f'(t - i*d)| along smooth imaginary nodal curves {I(d,t) = 0}
  away from critical points f'(z) = 0, while |f(t - i*d)|^2 = |f(t)|^2 + 2*int_0^d u*W_tilde_f(u,t) du
  holds identically. Specializing to Riemann's Xi(t - i*d) = xi(1/2 + d + i*t), global nodal
  monotonicity requires both critical-line Laguerre positivity L_1(Xi; tau_k) > 0 and the
  critical-point-free hypothesis Xi'(z) != 0 in d > 0 (equivalent to RH by Lagarias, 1999). Each
  real zero curve R(d,t) = 0 departs orthogonally from a simple zero gamma_k with vertex curvature
  kappa(gamma_k) = Xi''(gamma_k)/Xi'(gamma_k), matching the de Bruijn-Newman zero-velocity ratio of
  Csordas, Smith, and Varga (1994). Under canonical normalization W(d,t) = 16*int_0^infty y*sinh(2*d*y)*I_Wig(y,t) dy,
  transverse integration maps sinc_hyp(2*u*y) to sinc_hyp^2(d*y) with filter ratio tanh(d*y)/(d*y) in (0,1).
